    "title": "MiG-21 Farewell and Squadron Leader Priya Sharma's Role",
    "text": "• The MiG-21 is set to retire after over six decades of service in the Indian Air Force (IAF).\n• The official farewell event is scheduled for September 26 at Chandigarh Air Force Station.\n• The last of the MiG-21 jets belongs to number 23 Squadron, nicknamed 'Panthers'.\n• Air Chief Marshal AP Singh will fly the last sortie of the Squadron with the call sign 'Badal 3'.\n• Squadron Leader Priya Sharma will participate in the ceremonial MiG-21 flypast.\n• Sharma is a 2018 graduate of the Air Force Academy and the seventh woman fighter pilot in the IAF.\n• She hails from Jhunjhunu district in Rajasthan and followed her father's footsteps into the Air Force.\n• Sharma was the only woman fighter pilot in her batch and has served at multiple Air Force stations.\n• The farewell ceremony will feature a water cannon salute for the six jets upon landing.\n• The event will include a display by the IAF's elite skydiving team 'Akash Ganga' and a flypast of MiG-21 aircraft.\n• Fighter pilots will perform in the 'Badal' and 'Panther' formations during the flypast.\n• A commemorative postal stamp will be released to mark the retirement of the MiG-21 jets.\n• The MiG-21 played a significant role in India's 1965 and 1971 wars, the 1999 Kargil conflict, and the 2019 Balakot airstrikes.",
